{-
   A loop whose counter advances by a value rather than by a number.

   `reduceInductionVariables` carries the address of the element a walk is on and adds the stride to
   it, instead of rebuilding `base + i*stride` every iteration. The amount to add used to have to be
   a literal, because it was written as one in the latch; here it is `by`, which the loop does not
   compute, so the product is multiplied out in the preheader instead and the latch adds the value
   it left there.

   `Cell` is 24 bytes on purpose. An address whose scale is 1, 2, 4 or 8 goes into the SIB byte for
   nothing, and reducing one would buy a live pointer register in exchange for an addressing mode
   that was already free - so the pass declines those and this fixture would assert nothing about it.

   Two fields are read per element, which is the other half of the shape: `xs[i].a` and `xs[i].e` are
   one pointer and two displacements rather than two of each.

   The counter is `I64` because that is what makes the sequence reducible at all. A narrower one
   reaches an address through a sign extension, and `sext(i + by) == sext(i) + by` is only true where
   the narrow addition cannot wrap - which `stepCannotOverflow` proves by reading the stride as a
   number, so it has no answer for a stride that is a value. See lower_induction.h.
-}

import Native

data Cell {a: Int, b: Int, c: Int, d: Int, e: Int, f: Int}

fn strided(xs: [Cell], by: I64) -> Int:
    let &i = 0 :: I64
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    while i < 6:
        total = total + xs[i].a + xs[i].e
        i = i + by

    return total

-- The literal stride beside it, which is the case that already reduced. Both loops walk the same
-- array, so a pointer that advanced by the wrong amount is a different answer rather than a
-- different instruction count.
fn everyOther(xs: [Cell]) -> Int:
    let &i = 0 :: I64
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    while i < 6:
        total = total + xs[i].a + xs[i].e
        i = i + 2

    return total

fn main() -> Int:
    let &xs = [] :: [Cell]
    let &n = 0 :: Int

    while n < 6:
        push(xs, Cell {a: n, b: 0, c: 0, d: 0, e: n * 10, f: 0})
        n = n + 1

    -- 0, 2 and 4, each contributing `n + n*10`: 0 + 22 + 44.
    if strided(xs, 2) != 66 then return -1
    if everyOther(xs) != 66 then return -2

    -- Every element, so all six: 0 + 11 + 22 + 33 + 44 + 55.
    if strided(xs, 1) != 165 then return -3

    -- A stride past the end runs exactly once.
    if strided(xs, 9) != 0 then return -4

    return strided(xs, 2) + strided(xs, 3)
